.\" Not a v7 page: kctl(2) is this port's own call.  See BESM-6 NOTES.
.TH KCTL 2
.SH NAME
kctl, kgetsym  \-  read a kernel variable
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/kctl.h>
.PP
.B int kctl(const char *name, int op, void *buf, int len);
.PP
.B int kgetsym(const char *name);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kctl
reads the kernel's table of exported variables.
.I Name
selects one of them;
.I op
says what to report about it:
.TP
.B KCTL_GET
Copy the variable's current value.
.TP
.B KCTL_STAT
Copy a
.B "struct kctlstat" ,
which holds the variable's kernel
.I word
address in
.BR kc_addr ,
its size in bytes in
.BR kc_size ,
and
.B KCTLF_RD
in
.BR kc_flags .
.TP
.B KCTL_LIST
Copy the exported names, one per
.B KSYMLEN
bytes, NUL-padded.
.I Name
is not examined and may be a null pointer; divide the return value by
.B KSYMLEN
for the count.
.TP
.B KCTL_SET
Reserved.  It always fails; nothing may write a kernel variable today.
.PP
At most
.I len
bytes are copied into
.IR buf ,
and the number that arrived is returned \- the same rule
.IR read (2)
follows.  A
.I len
of zero copies nothing and returns the number of bytes
.IR available ,
which is how a caller sizes a buffer; a
.I len
smaller than that truncates rather than failing, so a caller that wants the whole
object compares the return value against what it asked for.
.PP
.I Kgetsym
is a library routine, not a system call: it performs a
.B KCTL_STAT
and returns
.BR kc_addr ,
or \-1.  It is the short form for the callers that want an address rather than a
value \- see BESM-6 NOTES.
.PP
No privilege is required.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mem(4), ps(1), dmesg(8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\-1 is returned and
.I errno
set on error.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.I Name
is not in the table.  An empty name matches nothing and is not treated specially.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I Op
is unknown or is
.BR KCTL_SET ,
or
.I name
has no terminating NUL within its first
.B KSYMLEN
bytes.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I Name
or
.I buf
is outside the process's address space.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B This call is not v7's, and exists because nlist(3) cannot work here.
Every other Unix finds a kernel variable by reading the symbol table of the kernel
image.  There is no kernel image on this root filesystem \- the simulator loads
.B unix
from the build host \- so there is nothing for
.IR nlist (3)
to open, and it is deliberately absent from this libc.  The kernel carries a small
hand-written table instead (kernel/ksym.c), and this call reads it.  RetroBSD met
the same wall, its kernel living in flash, and answered it through one
.IR sysctl (2)
node; there is no
.I sysctl
here and this is not one.
.PP
.B An address is a word address.
The kernel runs unmapped, so a kernel address is a physical word index.  To read the
variable through
.IR mem (4)
the offset is
.B "kc_addr * NBPW"
\- a byte offset, like every other file's.
.PP
.B Which operation to use is a real choice.
.B KCTL_GET
copies the value, so a program that reads nothing but fixed variables \-
.IR dmesg (8)
and
.IR iostat (1)
\- opens no device and needs no privilege.
.B KCTL_STAT
exists for the programs that cannot work that way:
.IR ps (1)
and
.IR pstat (8)
resolve
.B p_textp
into an index in
.BR text [],
and
.B u_ttyp
into one in
.BR sc [],
which is arithmetic against a base address that no amount of value copying supplies.
Those read on through /dev/kmem, and through /dev/mem for a u-area at
.BR p_addr ,
which lies above the unmapped reach.
.PP
.B A pointer-valued variable comes back raw.
.B Msgbufp
is the only one, and a
.B char *
here is a fat pointer: the word address is its low 15 bits.  Use
.B ptrword()
from <sys/param.h>.
.PP
.B The sizes in the table are the kernel's own sizeof.
A caller computes the matching count from the same <sys/param.h> the kernel was
built with \- there is no
.B nproc
variable to read, and there must not be one.
.PP
.B Under b6sim there is no kernel,
so every call fails with
.BR ENOENT ,
which is what a kernel with an empty table would say.  A program whose subject is
kernel state has to be tested under the booted kernel.
.SH ASSEMBLER
The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_kctl
\- with the first three arguments below r15 and
.I len
in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator and the error number in r14.  The
macro is in <sys/syscall.h>.  The number is 49, which v7 reserved and never used.
See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
